  "body": "### Why\n\nFollow-up to #1321 (part 2/2). The merged PR established GPU-specific kernel selection and a safe Flex-FA4 baseline. Three costs remain for packed, variable-length training:\n\n- Eager BlockMask construction can materialize large token-square intermediates.\n- Fixed-shape FLASH compilation specializes on changing sequence lengths and can exhaust the compilation cache.\n- Restoring full Q/K bounds for every affected auxiliary-mask call repeats work on complete tiles.\n\nThis PR compiles mask construction, reuses FLASH graphs across sequence lengths, and narrows the SM90 compatibility guard while preserving valid-token visibility and memory safety.\n\n### What does this PR do?\n\n1. **Compiled masks:** cache `torch.compile(create_block_mask)` and enable it by default in the Flex mask builder. `compile_block_mask=False` explicitly selects eager construction; HF's deprecated `_compile` flag is forced off in both paths.\n2. **Dynamic lengths:** compile FLASH with `dynamic=True, fullgraph=True`. Inside the wrapper, derive BlockMask bounds from normalized Q/K tensor shapes while preserving sparse buffers and the original predicate. Validate the caller's bounds before compilation. No compile-mode flag or dense eager fallback is introduced.\n3. **Tail-Q Q-only optimization:** for the pinned FA4 SM90 auxiliary-mask path, first establish that the current K tile is complete. Complete physical Q tiles delegate without an added callback guard; padded Q tiles clamp invalid logical Q indices before the callback and mask those rows afterward. Ragged/negative K tiles retain full native Q/K bounds, including reordered sparse lists. Physical Q extent accounts for native PackGQA.\n\n### Checklist Before Starting\n\n- Related PR: #1321, now merged. This PR targets `main` directly and contains only the optimization follow-up.\n- Title follows the `[ops, perf] feat: ...` format.\n\n### Test\n\nValidated with PyTorch 2.11.0+cu130, Transformers 5.16.1, flash-attn-4 4.0.0b16, and CUTLASS DSL 4.5.2. The local goal records contain the original logs, JUnit results, commands, and source provenance.\n\n| Validation | Result | Provenance |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| CPU attention + registry: `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES= python -m pytest tests/ops/attention tests/ops/base -q` | **463 passed, 70 skipped** | Current head `3800bf88d`, after moving onto merged #1321 |\n| H20 Flex adapter + FA4 compatibility suites | **183 passed** | Earlier optimization revision `395b4c1c4`, before the final parameter/docstring update and main-based consolidation |\n| Allocator-disabled compute-sanitizer memcheck | **47 passed, 109 deselected; 0 errors** | Initial PR2 split validation snapshot, 2026-10-19 |\n| Variable-length output/gradient and graph reuse | **2 graphs per case** across 12 distinct lengths plus repeats | Initial H20 validation; THD/BHSD × 28/4 and 7/1 head geometries, under a recompile limit of 4 |\n| Quality, all-file pre-commit, agent-document paths, strict Sphinx build (`-W`) | **Passed** | Current head `3800bf88d` |\n\nH20 coverage includes outputs and Q/K/V gradients against Math SDPA, asymmetric Q/K lengths, padded Q tiles, reordered ragged K tiles, shared-cache layout transitions, and combined outer static compilation. A separate compiled/eager BlockMask comparison at lengths 259 and 389, with 2D padding, matched all sparse tables and an independent visibility oracle.\n\nThe sanitizer run used `PYTORCH_NO_CUDA_MEMORY_CACHING=1`, `--target-processes all`, and an error exit code. Hardware validation ran on H20; SM80/SM100 numerics and NPU execution were not tested for this follow-up. GPU/memcheck results above are historical runs, not reruns of the final PR head. The main-based consolidation preserved the prior runtime/test/user-document file contents. No new end-to-end training throughput or convergence claim is made here.\n\nExtended existing CI-collected files under `tests/ops/attention/`; no new workflow entry is needed. The GPU unit workflow collects `tests/ops/` wholesale, with hardware guards for SM90-specific cases.\n\n### API and Usage Example\n\nKeep the selection introduced by #1321:\n\n```yaml\nmodel:\n  ops_implementation:\n    attn_implementation: flex_attention\n```\n\nCompiled mask construction and dynamic FLASH execution are automatic. The mask builder's explicit eager opt-out is:\n\n```python\nfrom veomni.ops.kernels.attention.mask.flex import flex_attention_mask_builder\n\nmask = flex_attention_mask_builder(\n    batch_size=1,\n    q_length=1024,\n    kv_length=1024,\n    device=\"cuda\",\n    compile_block_mask=False,\n)\n```\n\nFor variable-length custom masks, keep predicate code stable and store per-sample geometry in indexed metadata tensors. Shape-derived bounds do not normalize arbitrary captured Python integers or scalar-tensor captures. Outer whole-model `torch.compile(dynamic=True)` remains unsupported; outer static compilation has a dedicated regression. Other guards can still specialize, and cache exhaustion remains an explicit error.\n\n### Design & Code Changes\n\n- `attention/mask/flex.py`: cached compiled/eager mask builders, preserving architecture-specific block sizes and Ulysses length handling.\n- `standard/flex/_fa4.py`: tensor-derived bounds and dynamic full-graph FLASH execution, preserving exact-length validation.\n- `standard/flex/_fa4_sm90_compat.py`: K-completeness checks, physical tail-Q dispatch, Q-only safe indexing, and separate corrected-kernel cache namespaces.\n- Existing attention tests and `docs/transformers_v5/veomni_attention_interface.md`: compiled-mask equivalence, variable-length graph reuse, bounds regressions, usage, and supported compilation boundaries.\n\n### Checklist Before Submitting\n\n- Read the [Contribute Guide](https://github.com/ByteDance-Seed/VeOmni/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md).\n- Applied pre-commit checks and `make quality`.\n- Added/updated documentation.\n- No `tasks/` training scripts were moved or renamed.\n- Extended existing CI-collected tests; hardware and historical-result limits are recorded above.\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n<!-- coderabbit-ai-generated:v1 -->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **New Features**\n  * FlexAttention now supports compiled, cached mask generation by default, with an option for eager generation.\n  * Variable-length attention supports dynamic sequence lengths, sparse mask metadata, and graph reuse.\n  * Packed and broadcast attention inputs are handled more consistently across supported layouts.\n\n* **Bug Fixes**\n  * Improved handling of padded queries, incomplete key tiles, ragged sequences, and callback bounds.\n  * Added validation for inconsistent sequence bounds and clearer failures when compilation limits are exhausted.\n\n* **Documentation**\n  * Expanded guidance on dynamic compilation, caching, compatibility behavior, and safety considerations.\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->",
